digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56787-w
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper investigates whether the Hyaloperonospora arabidopsidis RXLR effector HaRxL10 targets the Arabidopsis circadian regulator CHE to perturb clock gene regulation and salicylic-acid-mediated immunity, including testing HaRxL10-CHE interaction, CHE stabilization, clock-transcriptome effects, and CHE-dependent immune outcomes.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Motivational evidence, effector-screening rationale, HaRxL10-CHE interaction evidence, CHE-dependent clock and immune phenotypes, and CHE stabilization mechanisms together support investigating HaRxL10 as a pathogen effector that targets CHE to alter circadian and SA-mediated immune regulation."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) If perturbing CHE can affect both core clock outputs and SA-mediated immunity, then CHE perturbation is a plausible shared route for changing circadian and immune outcomes."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Given CHE’s crucial role in salicylic-acid-mediated immunity, perturbing CHE can alter SA-mediated immune outcomes."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Because CHE interacts with ZTL and E3 ligases such as ZTL can control clock-component abundance, ZTL is a plausible regulator of CHE abundance or stability."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) HaRxL10 could stabilize CHE by interfering with ZTL-mediated CHE degradation."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Because CHE directly represses CCA1 and regulates core clock gene expression, altering CHE activity can perturb CCA1 expression and related circadian outputs."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) HaRxL10-CHE targeting motivates testing whether HaRxL10 affects circadian gene expression and SA-mediated immunity through CHE-dependent perturbation."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) HaRxL10 interaction with CHE is a plausible unifying explanation for CHE-dependent circadian and immune effects observed under HaRxL10."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Because plants couple immunity with the circadian clock, testing whether pathogens interfere with host circadian clocks is a valid research direction."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) Molecular and genetic data support that HaRxL10 interacts with CHE and can stabilize CHE by interfering with ZTL-mediated CHE degradation."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Because plants couple immunity with the circadian clock, identifying molecular events that link pathogen activity, clock regulation, and defense is an important research objective."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because more than 130 Hpa RXLR-type effectors are predicted and most remain functionally uncharacterized, functional or mechanistic characterization of specific Hpa RXLR effectors is warranted."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Because Hpa secretes effectors into plant cells to counteract plant defenses, and pathogen effector proteins can physically interact with host proteins, Hpa effectors are plausible candidates for host-protein interaction studies."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) HaRxL10 interaction with CHE makes HaRxL10 a plausible modulator of CHE-associated host regulatory or defense functions."];
  "nD1" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given infection-associated clock gene expression changes and the rule that specific mechanisms/targets must underlie such perturbations, therefore identifying pathogen triggers and targeted clock components is a concrete research gap."];
  "nR14_4" [label="(10,14,4) If pathogens deploy virulence effector proteins, then these effectors can physically interact with host proteins."];
  "nR14_5" [label="(10,14,5) If an effector physically interacts with a host protein, then it can modulate host defense-related functions."];
  "nR15_11" [label="(15,15,11) If RXLR effector virulence functions are largely unknown, then functional/mechanistic characterization of specific RXLR effectors is needed."];
  "nR18_9" [label="(19,18,9) If CHE is a clock component regulating core clock gene expression, then altering CHE activity can perturb circadian clock outputs."];
  "nR19_2" [label="(20,19,2) If the ubiquitin-proteasome system precisely degrades clock proteins, then E3 ligases like ZTL can control clock component abundance."];
  "nR20_6" [label="(21,20,6) If CHE functions as an activator within SA biosynthesis/immune signaling (e.g., via ICS1 activation), then perturbing CHE can alter SA-mediated immunity."];
  "nR6_15" [label="(3,6,15) If parasites can manipulate host clocks, then testing pathogen interference with host circadian clocks is a valid research direction."];
  "nS12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, Hpa secretes effectors into extracellular space or into plant cells to counteract plant defenses."];
  "nS15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, >130 Hpa RXLR-type effectors are predicted, but most functions are uncharacterized."];
  "nS17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, HaRxL10 interacts with several clock components including CHE in yeast, suggesting a CHE-dependent clock effect."];
  "nS19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, CHE is a central clock component that directly represses CCA1 expression."];
  "nS20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, CHE interacts with other clock components including TOC1 and ZTL."];
  "nS21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, CHE plays a crucial role in salicylic-acid (SA)-mediated immunity."];
  "nS22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, molecular/genetic data show HaRxL10 interacts with CHE and interferes with ZTL-mediated CHE degradation."];
  "nS25" [label="(25,0,0) Currently, HaRxL10 represses SA-mediated immunity and impacts multiple circadian outputs in a CHE-dependent manner."];
  "nS4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, plants couple immunity with the circadian clock to anticipate time-of-day infections."];
  "nS6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, prior studies show pathogen infection can alter expression patterns of plant circadian clock genes."];
  "nS7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, which pathogen mechanisms trigger clock perturbations and which clock components are targeted remain unknown."];
